Lesson 5
Data Visualization

The human brain understands data much faster through visual representations.

Brain's ability:
Most information is received through the eyes.

Advantages of Data Visualization:

1. Quick Understanding:
   Patterns, trends, and outliers can be identified immediately.

2. Comparison:
   Differences between groups can be easily observed.

3. Communication:
   It is easier to explain data to others.

4. Discovering Hidden Information:
   We can identify patterns that are difficult to notice in numerical tables.

==================================================

Six Main Types of Charts

1. Histogram
   Used to show the distribution of continuous numerical data.

2. Bar Chart
   Used to compare categorical (qualitative) data.

3. Pie Chart
   Used to show proportions or parts of a whole.

4. Line Chart
   Used to show changes over time.

5. Box Plot
   Used to display data distribution and outliers.

6. Stem and Leaf Plot
   Used to preserve and display the original data values.

==================================================

The Most Important Rule

Every chart has its own appropriate purpose.
Choosing the wrong chart may lead to misleading conclusions.

Before selecting a chart, ask these questions:

• What type of data do I have?
  (Numerical or categorical?)

• What do I want to show?
  (Distribution, comparison, trend, or proportion?)

• Who is the audience?
  (Experts or the general public?)

==================================================
Histogram
==================================================

Purpose:

A Histogram is used to display the distribution of continuous numerical data.

Characteristics:

• The bars touch each other.
• There are no gaps between the bars.
• This is because the data are continuous.
• The order of the intervals is important.

What can we learn from a Histogram?

• Shape:
  Is the distribution approximately symmetric or normal?

• Center:
  Where are most of the observations concentrated?

• Spread:
  Are the values widely spread or closely grouped?

• Outliers:
  Are there any unusual values?

Note:

Do not confuse a Histogram with a Bar Chart.

==================================================
Bar Chart
==================================================

Purpose:

A Bar Chart is used to compare categorical (qualitative) data.

Characteristics:

• The bars are separated.
• There are spaces between the bars.
• Categories are independent of each other.
• The order is usually not important.
  (They may be arranged alphabetically or from largest to smallest.)

Note:

A Bar Chart can be drawn vertically or horizontally.

A Histogram is normally drawn only vertically.

==================================================
Pie Chart
==================================================

Purpose:

A Pie Chart is used to show how each category contributes to the whole.

Rules:

• The total of all slices must equal 100%.
• It is most effective when there are only a few categories.
  (Usually no more than 5 to 7 categories.)

Advantages:

• Clearly shows the relationship between the whole and its parts.
• Easy for most people to understand.
• Commonly used in reports, presentations, and advertisements.

Disadvantages:

• Too many categories make the chart difficult to read.
• Similar-sized slices are difficult to compare.
• It cannot show changes over time.
• Exact numerical values are difficult to read.

Note:

If your goal is simply to compare values, a Bar Chart is usually a better choice.

A Pie Chart should only be used to show proportions of a whole.

==================================================
Line Chart
==================================================

Purpose:

A Line Chart is used to show changes over time.

It is useful for showing:

• Trends
• Continuous changes
• Seasonal patterns

Axes:

X-axis = Time (Day, Month, Year)

Y-axis = Value

==================================================
Box Plot
==================================================

Purpose:

A Box Plot is used to display:

• Data distribution
• Center
• Spread
• Outliers

in a single graph.

It is especially useful for comparing several groups.

What can we learn from a Box Plot?

• Median
• Quartiles
• Spread
• Outliers

==================================================
Stem and Leaf Plot
==================================================

Purpose:

A Stem and Leaf Plot is used for small and medium-sized data sets.

Main Feature:

The original data values are preserved.

Unlike a Histogram, the original numbers are not lost.

Each number is divided into two parts:

Stem = Leading digit(s)

Leaf = Last digit

What can we learn from a Stem and Leaf Plot?

• Original values
• Distribution
• Center
• Median

Advantages:

• Keeps the original data.
• Easy to draw by hand.
• Very useful for small data sets.
• Provides detailed information.

Disadvantages:

• Not suitable for large data sets.
• Less visually attractive.
• Not commonly used in presentations.
• Rarely used in modern statistical practice.

In practice, Stem and Leaf Plots are mainly used for teaching purposes.

For real-world analysis, Histograms and statistical software are usually preferred.

==================================================
The Most Important Rule

A good chart should be:

• Accurate
• Clear
• Simple

==================================================
Practical Examples

Example 1

Hospital – Patients' Ages

Objective:

To determine which age group has the largest number of patients.

Best Choice:

Histogram

Reason:

Age is continuous numerical data, and we want to study its distribution.

--------------------------------------------------

Example 2

Company – Department Expenses

Objective:

To compare the expenses of different departments.

Best Choice:

Bar Chart

Reason:

Departments are categorical data, and comparison is required.

--------------------------------------------------

Example 3

Government – Annual Population Growth

Objective:

To show population changes over a period of 20 years.

Best Choice:

Line Chart

Reason:

The data represent changes over time.

--------------------------------------------------

Example 4

School – Students' Family Income

Objective:

To show the proportion of students from different income groups.

Best Choice:

Pie Chart

Reason:

The purpose is to show parts of a whole.

--------------------------------------------------

Example 5

Factory – Product Quality of Three Production Lines

Objective:

To compare the quality of three production lines.

Best Choice:

Box Plot

Reason:

It allows comparison of multiple groups while displaying distribution and outliers.

--------------------------------------------------

Example 6

Teacher – Analysis of Scores for 15 Students

Objective:

To examine the details while preserving the original scores.

Best Choice:

Stem and Leaf Plot

Reason:

The data set is small, and every value is important.

==================================================

Note:

The same data can often be displayed using different types of charts.

The most important thing is to choose the chart that best matches your objective.